sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Input as Input x
    participant Linear as Linear calculation
    participant Params as Weights w and bias b
    participant Loss as Binary cross-entropy
    participant Target as Target y
    participant Auto as Autograd

    Note over Input,Target: One example: x = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], y = [0, 0, 0]<br/>w has shape [5, 3] and b has shape [3]<br/>Both contain zeros
    Input->>Linear: x, shape [5]: five input features
    Params->>Linear: w and b, both require gradients
    Linear->>Linear: x @ w: combine all five inputs for each of three outputs
    Note over Linear: x @ w = [0, 0, 0], shape [3]
    Linear->>Linear: Add b: z = x @ w + b
    Linear-->>Loss: Logits z = [0, 0, 0], shape [3]
    Target->>Loss: y = [0, 0, 0], shape [3]
    Loss->>Loss: Compare each logit with its corresponding target<br/>sigmoid(0) = 0.5 for each output
    Note over Loss: Individual losses = [0.6931, 0.6931, 0.6931]
    Loss->>Loss: Average the three individual losses
    Loss-->>Input: Scalar loss = 0.6931, shape []
    Note over Loss,Auto: Autograd tracks the operations involving w and b<br/>so loss.backward() can traverse them later
